From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 13:25:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] mkfs: allow building a filesystem image in a regular file

`mkfs -F ufs` can only target a disk today.  Pointed at an ordinary
file it stops before writing anything:

    Can not determine partition size: Inappropriate ioctl for device

That is a real limitation rather than an oversight of the interface --
the size, the mount interlock and the open all assume a device -- but
it is a useful thing to be able to do.  Building a root filesystem
image in a file is how you populate a VM, an installer or a boot
archive without first finding a spare disk to scribble on, and it is
what `newfs(8)` on the BSDs already allows.

Three places assume a device, and each needs the ordinary-file case
spelled out:

  - `get_device_size()` asks the driver via `DKIOCGMEDIAINFO`.  A file
    has no media to interrogate, but it does have a length, so fall
    back to `fstat()` and divide by `DEV_BSIZE`.  Anything that is
    neither keeps the historical answer of 0, and so still reports
    "cannot determine partition size".

  - The `MNTTAB` interlock exists to refuse to write over a mounted
    filesystem, and it works by looking the target up as a block device
    in the mount table.  A regular file is never a mounted block
    device, so the check cannot apply to one; skip it.  The open that
    followed it is not part of the interlock and now sits in its own
    block so that it still happens either way.

  - `creat(2)` truncates.  On a device that is a no-op, but on a file
    it discards the very length the geometry was just computed from,
    leaving nothing to write into and failing with

        short read (0 of 512 bytes) on sector 131071

    Open without `O_TRUNC` instead.

A new `fd_is_regular_file()` answers the question for all three, so
there is one definition of what "not a device" means here.

Behaviour against a real disk is unchanged: every new path is reached
only once the device-specific one has already failed or been found
inapplicable.

Verified by building a 64MB image on a file and checking that both the
primary and the backup superblock carry FS_MAGIC at the offsets mkfs
reports:

    $ mkfs -F ufs test.img 131072
    test.img:	131072 sectors in 22 cylinders of 48 tracks, 128 sectors
	    64.0MB in 2 cyl groups (13 c/g, 39.00MB/g, 18624 i/g)
    super-block backups (for fsck -F ufs -o b=#) at:
     32, 80032,

Assisted-by: Claude Code (Claude Opus 5)
---
 usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c | 65 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 62 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c b/usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c
index 821aad8ee5..82c8ca0576 100644
--- a/usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c
+++ b/usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c
@@ -417,6 +417,7 @@ static void range_check(long *varp, char *name, long minimum,
     long maximum, long def_val, int user_supplied);
 static void range_check_64(diskaddr_t *varp, char *name, uint64_t minimum,
     uint64_t maximum, uint64_t def_val, int user_supplied);
+static int fd_is_regular_file(int fd);
 static daddr32_t alloc(int size, int mode);
 static diskaddr_t get_max_size(int fd);
 static long get_max_track_size(int fd);
@@ -1407,7 +1408,16 @@ retry_alternate_logic:
 		}
 	}
 
-	if (!Nflag) {
+	/*
+	 * The checks below exist to stop mkfs writing over a filesystem that
+	 * is currently mounted, and they work by looking the target up as a
+	 * block device in the mount table.  A regular file is never a mounted
+	 * block device, so none of it applies to one -- and MNTTAB itself is
+	 * an illumos mntfs mount, which a build host running some other system
+	 * has no counterpart for.  Skip the interlock in that case; the open
+	 * below still happens either way.
+	 */
+	if (!Nflag && !fd_is_regular_file(fsi)) {
 		/*
 		 * Check if MNTTAB is trustable
 		 */
@@ -1459,8 +1469,21 @@ retry_alternate_logic:
 			    special);
 			lockexit(32);
 		}
+	}
 
-		fso = (grow) ? open64(fsys, O_WRONLY) : creat64(fsys, 0666);
+	if (!Nflag) {
+		/*
+		 * creat(2) truncates.  On a device that is a no-op, but on a
+		 * regular file it throws away the very length that the
+		 * geometry above was derived from, leaving nothing to write
+		 * into.  Open without O_TRUNC in that case.
+		 */
+		if (grow)
+			fso = open64(fsys, O_WRONLY);
+		else if (fd_is_regular_file(fsi))
+			fso = open64(fsys, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT, 0666);
+		else
+			fso = creat64(fsys, 0666);
 		if (fso < 0) {
 			saverr = errno;
 			(void) fprintf(stderr,
@@ -2383,13 +2406,49 @@ grow50:
 	return (0);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Is this descriptor an ordinary file rather than a device?
+ *
+ * Several things mkfs does only make sense against a disk: interrogating a
+ * partition table, consulting the mount table, asking the driver for its
+ * transfer size.  None of them apply when the target is a file, and some of
+ * them fail outright, so it is worth being able to ask.
+ */
+static int
+fd_is_regular_file(int fd)
+{
+	struct stat64 st;
+
+	return (fstat64(fd, &st) == 0 && S_ISREG(st.st_mode));
+}
+
+/*
+ * A regular file has no media to interrogate, so DKIOCGMEDIAINFO fails on
+ * one.  Its size is simply how long it is, which makes it possible to build
+ * a filesystem image in a file rather than only on a disk -- the same thing
+ * newfs(8) on the BSDs will do, and what a build system wants.
+ *
+ * Anything else that is not a disk keeps the historical answer of 0, which
+ * the caller reports as "cannot determine partition size".
+ */
+static diskaddr_t
+get_regular_file_size(int fd)
+{
+	struct stat64 st;
+
+	if (!fd_is_regular_file(fd) || fstat64(fd, &st) == -1)
+		return (0);
+
+	return ((diskaddr_t)(st.st_size / DEV_BSIZE));
+}
+
 static diskaddr_t
 get_device_size(int fd)
 {
 	struct dk_minfo	disk_info;
 
 	if ((ioctl(fd, DKIOCGMEDIAINFO, (caddr_t)&disk_info)) == -1)
-		return (0);
+		return (get_regular_file_size(fd));
 
 	return (disk_info.dki_capacity);
 }
